// JavaScript implementation facet. Events are declared in input.kz (merged at
// import); this file holds the |js proc bodies.
//
// FRONTIER — no `|js` body can make this test pass, because the blocker is in
// the FLOW, not in a proc. The call site is
//
//     ~get-data(): r[mutable] |> mutate-and-print(ptr: &r)
//
// and `&r` is Zig address-of syntax written as a Koru argument. Arg text is
// carried through verbatim, so the emitted JavaScript contains `const ptr =
// &r;` and dies at parse. Moving it would mean editing `input.kz`, which is
// the Zig facet and the Zig baseline both.
//
// Deeper than the syntax: what this test pins is mutation THROUGH a pointer —
// the event declares `ptr: *i32` and does `ptr.* += 1`, and the caller then
// observes the mutated `r`. JavaScript has no address-of and no out-parameter
// for a number. The body below prints the right line for the wrong reason
// (it computes `ptr + 1` instead of mutating), and that is exactly why this
// should NOT be counted as ported: a JS facet here could only ever imitate the
// output while dropping the property under test.
//
// The `[mutable]` binding annotation itself — the parser-level thing the test
// name refers to — is host-neutral and would port fine. It is the pointer that
// does not.

~proc get-data|js {
    return 42;
}

~proc mutate-and-print|js {
    console.log(`Value: ${ptr + 1}`);
}
